<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lullaby · c. 1780 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Au clair de la lune</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old French folk song about a moonlit night. In 1860 it became the first music ever recorded.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French popular and children's song</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>18th century; printed by 1820</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lullaby</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional (a French folk song of the 18th century; printed by 1820))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/au-clair-de-la-lune">Au clair de la lune</a>&quot; (&quot;By the Light of the Moon&quot;) is a French folk song of the 18th century whose composer and lyricist are not known. Its first verse appears in print as early as 1820, in Les Voitures versées; four verses were published in the 1858 collection Chants et chansons populaires de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the 1870 collection Chansons et rondes enfantines kept only the first two. Some sources report that the word for &quot;pen&quot; in the first verse was once an old word for &quot;light&quot; or &quot;lamp&quot;, which fits the song's setting better. Much of the text carries sexual double meanings.</p><p>Its simple melody made it a favourite for teaching beginners on an instrument, and composers returned to it often. Ferdinand Hérold wrote piano variations on it in 1820 and Muzio Clementi a fantasia in 1821. Claude Debussy built his song &quot;Pierrot&quot; (1882) on it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camille-saint-saens">Camille Saint-Saëns</a> quoted its opening in &quot;The Fossils&quot; in The Carnival of the Animals (1886), and Erik Satie quoted it in Sports et divertissements (1914). Samuel Barber, Marc Blitzstein and Frank Martin made settings of their own in the 20th century, Martin's for one of his children to practise octaves. In 1964 the singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> Gall recorded it with new words that made it a love song.</p><p>The song has a place in the history of sound recording. On 9 April 1860 Édouard-Léon Scott de Martinville recorded himself singing its beginning on his phonautograph. It is the earliest recognisable recording of a human voice and of music. In 2008 researchers at the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory converted the recording digitally into sound.</p><p>It was long sung to children. A story in The Ladies Pocket Magazine of 1835 describes it as sung every night by the little boys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. In his memoir Witness (1952), Whittaker Chambers remembered his mother singing it to his brother at bedtime, and how its sad turn troubled him as a small child. It also appears in F. Scott Fitzgerald's Tender Is the Night, in Maxwell Anderson and William March's play Bad Seed (1957) and in Lauren Groff's story collection <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> (2018).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A small night scene under the moon: one person comes to a neighbour's door in the dark, asking for help to write a note because their light has gone out, and is not let in. The tune is simple and calm, which suits a bedtime song, though later verses play on double meanings meant for adult listeners.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Au clair de la lune,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the light of the moon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mon ami Pierrot,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My friend Pierrot,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Prête-moi ta plume</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lend me your pen</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour écrire un mot.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To write a word.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ma chandelle est morte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My candle is dead,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Je n'ai plus de feu ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I have no more fire;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ouvre-moi ta porte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Open your door to me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour l'amour de Dieu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the love of God.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Au clair de la lune,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the light of the moon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pierrot répondit :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Pierrot answered:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">« Je n'ai pas de plume,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“I have no pen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Je suis dans mon lit.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I am in my bed.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Va chez la voisine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Go to the neighbour's,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Je crois qu'elle y est,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I think she is there,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Car dans sa cuisine</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For in her kitchen</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">On bat le briquet. »</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Someone is striking the flint.”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Au clair de la lune,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the light of the moon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L'aimable Lubin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The amiable Lubin</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Frappe chez la brune,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Knocks at the dark-haired girl's,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ell' répond soudain :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She answers at once:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">— Qui frapp' de la sorte ?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>— Who knocks like that?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il dit à son tour :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He says in his turn:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">— Ouvrez votre porte</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>— Open your door</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le dieu d'amour !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the god of love!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Au clair de la lune,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the light of the moon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">On n'y voit qu'un peu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One sees only a little.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">On chercha la plume,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They looked for the pen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">On chercha le feu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They looked for the fire.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">En cherchant d'la sorte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Looking in that way,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Je n'sais c'qu'on trouva ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I don't know what they found;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais je sais qu'la porte</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But I know that the door</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sur eux se ferma…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Closed on them...</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional (18th century); as quoted in Wikipedia's article (French Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Au_clair_de_la_lune" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>line 2.8: 'battre le briquet', to strike a fire steel against a flint to make a light.</li><li>line 3.3: 'la brune', a brunette: the dark-haired girl (the neighbour).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="IYLTc3tGdzc" data-title="Au clair de la lune: Sung as a children's song"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Sung as a children's song</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Comptines et chansons. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IYLTc3tGdzc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variations for piano in E-flat major (Ferdinand Hérold)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1820</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Les voitures versées (Boieldieu)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1820</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations in an opera</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fantasia, Op. 48 (Muzio Clementi)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1821</span><span class="sg-chain-h">fantasia on the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Recording by Édouard-Léon Scott de Martinville</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1860</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first recorded music, on a phonautograph</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variations (Charles-Lucien Lambert)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1860</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pierrot, from Quatre chansons de jeunesse (Claude Debussy)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1882</span><span class="sg-chain-h">based on the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Carnival of the Animals, &quot;The Fossils&quot; (Camille Saint-Saëns)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1886</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sports et divertissements, &quot;Le flirt&quot; (Erik Satie)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1914</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">H-35: Au clair de la lune, a modern setting of an old folk tune (Samuel Barber)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1926</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variations sur Au clair de la lune (Marc Blitzstein)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1928</span><span class="sg-chain-h">orchestration</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Au clair de la lune, piano duet (Frank Martin)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1955</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting with three variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Au clair de la lune (France Gall)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1964</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words as a love song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Au clair de la lune (Fred Momotenko)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2008</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tribute score for vocal ensemble and surround audio</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">For the God of Love, For the Love of God (Lauren Groff)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2018</span><span class="sg-chain-h">story titled after the song's words</span></li></ul></section>
